==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|canner}}; equivalent to {{suffix|en|can|er}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-canner.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ænə(r)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Someone or something which [[can]]s.
#* '''1937''', ''Technology Review'' (volume 40, page 100)
#*: One machine in which Mr. Taylor takes special pride is a salmon '''canner''', which engulfs a whole salmon, decapitates and decaudates it, skins it, blows out its viscera, cuts it into pieces, deposits them in the can, sterilizes them {{...}}
# A large [[pot]] used for processing [[jar]]s when [[preserving]] [[food]], either in a boiling [[water]] [[bath]] or by capturing [[steam]] to elevate the [[pressure]] and [[temperature]].
# {{lb|en|US|slang}} Someone who lives off [[w:Container-deposit legislation|container deposit]] refunds from recycling.
#* '''2007''', Jon Mooallem, ''[http://www.nytimes.com/2007/05/27/magazine/27Bottle-t.html The Unintended Consequences of Hyperhydration],'' {{w|New York Times}}, May 27, 2007, [http://www.nytimes.com/2007/05/27/magazine/27Bottle-t.html?pagewanted=7 p. 7]:
#*: Yet many '''canners''' told me that they can easily earn a daily wage of 20 or 30 dollars; each then recycles upward of 600 containers every day.
#* '''2009''', {{w|Camilo Jose Vergara}}, ''[http://www.slate.com/id/2236946/ 125th and Lex: The most complicated, disturbing, and lively intersection in New York City. A photo essay.]'', {{w|Slate.com}}, December 3, 2009:
#*: Among the crowds are ordinary working people shopping at the Pathmark, as well as down-and-out "'''canners'''" bringing their cans and bottles to the recycling station on East 124th Street.
# An [[animal]] yielding [[inferior]] [[meat]] best suited to [[can]]ning.
#: {{cot|en|cutter}}
#* '''1905''', United States. Bureau of Corporations, ''Report of the Commissioner of Corporations on the Beef Industry'' (page 89)
#*: Bulls and cows used for breeding, when finally sent to market, are inferior for dressed-beef production. Bulls are demanded especially for sausage and similar products. Cows are largely used as cutters and '''canners''' {{...}}

